---
title: Quick start
description: A command, a query and an event wired end to end with source-generated registration.
---

This walks through a minimal but complete setup: one command, one query, one event, and the
startup code that registers all of them without a single hand-written registration call.

## Step 1 — Install

```bash
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare --prerelease
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare.SourceGenerator --prerelease
```

## Step 2 — Declare a command and its handler

A command is a message that *does* something. It may or may not produce a result; use
`ICommand<TResult>` when it does.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public CreateProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Two things worth noticing:

- The handler returns **`ValueTask<TResult>`**. A handler that completes synchronously
  allocates nothing.
- The cancellation token arrives on the **context**, not as a separate parameter. There is
  no way to forget to thread it through.

## Step 3 — Declare a query and its handler

A query *asks* something and always produces a result.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<Product?>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public GetProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Step 4 — Declare an event and its handlers

An event is broadcast: every matching handler runs, and there may be none.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name) : IEvent;

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _mailer.SendAsync(@event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}

public sealed class UpdateSearchIndex : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _index.AddAsync(@event.Id, @event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Step 5 — Register everything

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(q => q.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterGenerated()));
```

That is the whole registration. `RegisterGenerated()` does not exist in any NuGet package —
the source generator emits it into *your* compilation, already containing every handler it
found. Add a handler anywhere in the project and the next build picks it up.

{% aside type="tip" %}
`using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;` is required — that is the namespace the generator emits
into. A missing `RegisterGenerated()` is almost always a missing using.
{% /aside %}

## Step 6 — Dispatch

Inject the mediator interface for the module you need. They are separate on purpose: a
component that only reads does not get the ability to send commands.

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (
    CreateProduct command,
    ICommandMediator commands,
    IEventMediator events) =>
{
    var id = await commands.SendAsync(command);
    await events.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, command.Name));
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});

app.MapGet("/products/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, IQueryMediator queries) =>
{
    var product = await queries.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
    return product is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(product);
});
```

| Module | Interface | Call |
|---|---|---|
| Commands | `ICommandMediator` | `SendAsync(command)` |
| Queries | `IQueryMediator` | `QueryAsync(query)`, `StreamAsync(streamQuery)` |
| Events | `IEventMediator` | `PublishAsync(@event)` |

## Step 7 — Add a cross-cutting concern

Interceptors attach to the pipeline without touching handlers. A pre-interceptor registered
against a base type or interface applies to every message assignable to it:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Attributes;

[Weight(100)]                                  // higher weight runs earlier
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogCommands> _logger;

    public LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);   // pass the message through
    }
}
```

No registration needed — the generator finds it exactly like a handler.

{% aside type="note" %}
A pre-interceptor returns the message rather than `void`, because it is allowed to *rewrite*
it: whatever you return is what the next stage sees. Returning the message unchanged, as
above, is the pass-through case. See
[Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors).
{% /aside %}

